00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.970
The road to making the Zootopia movie that
we know and love today started with a number

00:01:04.970 --> 00:01:10.229
of ideas which director Byron Howard proposed
to Disney's chief creative officer John Lasseter.

00:01:10.229 --> 00:01:14.009
For example, there was a pitch to adapt Alexandre
Dumas' classic novel The Three Musketeers

00:01:14.009 --> 00:01:16.299
with mice in the starring roles.

00:01:16.299 --> 00:01:20.649
What the ideas that Byron Howard pitched had
in common was that they all involved walking,

00:01:20.649 --> 00:01:22.359
talking animals who wore clothes.

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:26.950
In fact, both Byron Howard and John Lasseter
shared a love of these types of animation

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.969
with the Disney classic Robin Hood being a
particular inspiration for Howard.

00:01:31.969 --> 00:01:37.729
"The Island of Doctor Meow" was another story
that was considered by the filmmakers.

00:01:37.729 --> 00:01:43.270
This story featured a 6-foot tall mad scientist
cat that possessed a savage serum which could

00:01:43.270 --> 00:01:45.470
transform children into animals.

00:01:45.470 --> 00:01:50.970
The concept was pitched as a Roger Corman
1960s style B movie with the title and story

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:56.130
drawing inspiration from the science fiction
novel The Island of Dr Moreau by H.G. Wells

00:01:56.130 --> 00:02:00.710
in which Dr Moreau, who lives on an island,
creates human-like creatures by experimenting

00:02:00.710 --> 00:02:02.310
on animals.

00:02:02.310 --> 00:02:08.490
There was also a Guardians of the Galaxy-style
movie pitched called Pug: The Bounty Hunter.

00:02:08.490 --> 00:02:13.220
The story was set in outer space and was about
a team of animals hunting for other animals

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:16.130
in space.

00:02:16.130 --> 00:02:20.170
After considering those stories, Howard said
that Lasseter loved the ideas and wanted to

00:02:20.170 --> 00:02:25.500
combine "the quirky quality of the 1960s world
[with] the animal characters from the space

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:31.690
pug concept" which led Howard to develop his
next concept: a spy movie called Savage Seas.

00:02:31.690 --> 00:02:36.110
Savage Seas was planned as a spoof of James
Bond and starred a jackrabbit spy called Jack

00:02:36.110 --> 00:02:41.210
Savage who worked at a place like MI6 where
he had a tiny mouse boss, a bit like Judy

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:42.210
Dench.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.990
The movie's first act was set in an animal
city which Jack Savage left for the South

00:02:45.990 --> 00:02:47.510
Seas in the second act.

00:02:47.510 --> 00:02:53.070
Byron Howard even had titles ready for a whole
series of related movies such as Savage Earth,

00:02:53.070 --> 00:02:57.850
The Savage Land and The Savage Times, however
what he found was that people weren't so keen

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.080
on the spy element but wanted to see more
of the animal city from the first act.

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.030
It was at this point that Howard decided to
set the whole movie in the animal city, keeping

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:11.480
the bunny character and adding a fox as he
felt it would "make a great buddy dynamic

00:03:11.480 --> 00:03:13.980
[… as] foxes are natural predators of rabbits."

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:20.740
Once the filmmakers decided to focus the film
on the animal city of Zootopia, the idea was

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:25.320
then developed with a much darker story than
the movie we know today and it centred around

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.650
the character of Nick Wilde as the lead.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:32.120
In this early story, there was a much more
overt level of prejudice from the prey population

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:36.590
against the minority predator population who
were forced to wear tame collars.

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:41.070
The film had a dystopian feel about it, with
the tame collars acting to control predators

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.650
by shocking them whenever they became excited
or violent.

00:03:44.650 --> 00:03:49.730
One particular sub-story involved Nick setting
up a secret amusement park called Wild Times

00:03:49.730 --> 00:03:53.790
just for predators where they would be able
to remove their tame collars and indulge some

00:03:53.790 --> 00:03:56.880
of their natural instincts without being electro-shocked.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.870
And if you'd like know more about the designs
for Wild Times, I talk about that in my video

00:04:00.870 --> 00:04:04.860
about Zootopia's deleted scenes which you
can click here to watch.

00:04:04.860 --> 00:04:09.130
In this version of the movie, Judy was also
a less sympathetic character who initially

00:04:09.130 --> 00:04:11.150
supported the collaring of predators.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:15.680
She was a tough police officer who chased
after Nick when he escaped from the ZPD after

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:16.970
being put in prison.

00:04:16.970 --> 00:04:20.849
By the way in the final film, this chase was
replaced with Judy chasing Weaselton through

00:04:20.849 --> 00:04:22.240
Little Rodentia.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:26.960
Eventually Judy came to realise the harsh
truth about the taming collar when she secretly

00:04:26.960 --> 00:04:31.870
witnessed a taming party for Koslov's son
Morris, a ceremony in which the 5-year old

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:36.669
polar bear cub receives a taming collar for
the first time and as he gets excited he also

00:04:36.669 --> 00:04:39.389
gets shocked by the collar for the first time.

00:04:39.389 --> 00:04:43.990
And it was after screenings with the Disney
story trust and later at Pixar that the filmmakers

00:04:43.990 --> 00:04:48.550
realised that although the story was interesting,
Zootopia was just too dark a place for the

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.960
audience to like.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:56.129
This realisation came late in 2014 with the
film due out early 2016, which meant the filmmakers

00:04:56.129 --> 00:04:58.380
had little over a year to change course.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:02.449
They threw out the tame collar concept and
made Judy the lead character.

00:05:02.449 --> 00:05:06.550
The story of prejudice was still told but
in a much less overt way and through the eyes

00:05:06.550 --> 00:05:11.849
of Judy experiencing stereotypes as she worked
the case of the missing otter, later discovering

00:05:11.849 --> 00:05:13.599
her own bias as well.

